 however. His career-long investigation into image…","people":[],"isFree":false,"priceFrom":null,"imageURL":"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/GbJYcu_VlD0/maxresdefault.jpg","sourceURL":"https://whitney.org/exhibitions/lichtenstein","sourceName":"Whitney Museum of American Art","isFestival":false,"showtimes":[],"soldOut":null,"fewLeft":null,"reviews":[{"outlet":"The New York Times","title":"Alex Da Corte Takes On Roy Lichtenstein and Marisol","url":"https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/28/arts/design/pop-art-dacorte-lichtenstein-marisol.html","date":"2026-10-28"}],"selected":true,"spotNote":null,"artistPick":false,"goodPick":2},{"id":"artlogic-ny-lehmann-maupin-calida-rawles-parallax","title":"Parallax","category":"art","venueID":"osm-n10859974153","start":"2026-10-14T22:00:00Z","end":"2026-11-14T22:59:59Z","openingNight":null,"summary":"In her latest exhibition of new paintings, Calida Rawles explores parallax: how changing our point of view can change the way we see the same moment. Known for merging hyperrealism with poetic abstraction, Rawles situates Black subjects within dynamic, fluid environments, using water as both a vital material and a historically charged space through which questions of identity, memory, healing, and belonging come into focus.\n\nThis new exhibition centers on a series of three paintings based on photographs Rawles took of her youngest daughter in a pool. Captured within seconds of one another as Rawles moved around her in the water, the photographs record essentially the same moment from…","people":[],"isFree":true,"priceFrom":null,"imageURL":null,"sourceURL":"https://www.lehmannmaupin.com/exhibitions/calida-rawles-parallax/","sourceName":"Lehmann Maupin","isFestival":false,"showtimes":[],"soldOut":null,"fewLeft":null,"reviews":[],"selected":true,"spotNote":null,"artistPick":false,"goodPick":0},{"id":"artlogic-ny-international-center-of-photography-im-so-happy-you-are-here-japanese-women-photographers-1950s-","title":"I'm So Happy You Are Here: Japanese Women Photographers from the 1950s to Now","category":"art","venueID":"osm-n9371206934","start":"2026-10-14T22:00:00Z","end":"2027-01-11T22:59:59Z","openingNight":null,"summary":"I’m So Happy You Are Here: Japanese Women Photographers from the 1950s to Now reconsiders and expands on the history of Japanese photography through the work twenty-eight intergenerational artists from the 1950s to today. Presented in partnership with Aperture and curated by Pauline Vermare, Lesley A. Martin, and Takeuchi Mariko, the exhibition includes work by Fujioka Aya, Hara Mikiko, Hiromix, Ishikawa Mao, Ishiuchi Miyako, Katayama Mari, Kawauchi Rinko, Komatsu Hiroko, Kon Michiko, Nagashima Yurie, Narahashi Asako, Ninagawa Mika, Nishimura Tamiko, Noguchi Rika, Nomura Sakiko, Okabe Momo, Okanoue Toshiko, Onodera Yuki, Sawada Tomoko, Shiga Lieko, Sugiura Kunié, Suzuki Nozomi, Tawada Yuki…","people":[],"isFree":true,"priceFrom":null,"imageURL":null,"sourceURL":"https://www.icp.org/exhibitions/im-so-happy-you-are-here-japanese-women-photographers-1950s-now/","sourceName":"International Center of Photography","isFestival":false,"showtimes":[],"soldOut":null,"fewLeft":null,"reviews":[],"selected":true,"spotNote":null,"artistPick":false,"goodPick":1},{"id":"artlogic-ny-international-center-of-photography-laia-abril-rape-and-institutional-failure","title":"Laia Abril: On Rape and Institutional Failure","category":"art","venueID":"osm-n9371206934","start":"2026-10-14T22:00:00Z","end":"2027-01-11T22:59:59Z","openingNight":null,"summary":"In On Rape and Institutional Failure, an exhibition centered on the second chapter of her ongoing project A History of Misogyny, Laia Abril examines different forms of violence against women throughout history.Weaving together images, compiled testimonies, archival materials, select objects, and installations with sound and video, the exhibition investigates how gender-based stereotypes, coupled with worldwide prejudices and misconceptions, have contributed t